Declarative state for the RKE2 cluster, reconciled by Flux from the internal
GitLab. Nothing here reaches the internet: every image resolves to
`{{ gitops_source_registry_host }}` through the containerd rewrite configured
on each node, so manifests keep upstream image names and stay portable.

Every file below — including this one — is rendered by the `gitops_source`
Ansible role and pushed here. An edit made in GitLab is not the source of
anything; it survives until the next `ansible-playbook playbooks/gitops.yml`
and is then overwritten without warning. The one exception is
`clusters/dev-lo/flux-system/`: that folder is owned by the Flux CLI,
regenerated by the `gitops_bootstrap` role via `flux install --export` and
`flux create ... --export`, and committed in its own commit. Ansible's
scoped commits never touch it, and the bootstrap role force-recreates it
from the CLI on every run, so the two ownerships stay cleanly separated.

The source is `ansible/roles/gitops_source/templates/cluster-state/` in the
automation repository. Change it there, run the playbook, and the commit
appears here.

This is not bureaucracy. The lab is built on the premise that the whole
internal environment can be rebuilt from the automation repository alone, and
`repo01` — this GitLab — is one of the things that gets rebuilt. State that
exists only here is state that does not survive that.

```text
clusters/dev-lo/         Flux entry point: the cluster's own Kustomizations
  flux-system/           Flux's components and the GitRepository/Kustomization
                         pair that points at this repository — generated by
                         the Flux CLI and committed by gitops_bootstrap, not
                         rendered by gitops_source
  infrastructure.yaml    infra-controllers -> infra-configs, in that order
  apps.yaml              workloads, after the infrastructure is ready
infrastructure/
  controllers/           cert-manager, Longhorn, ESO, OpenBao, Sealed Secrets
  configs/               ClusterIssuers, StorageClasses, IPPools — objects that
                         need a controller's CRDs to exist first
apps/                    Keycloak, Garage, observability
```

The `controllers`/`configs` split exists because a `ClusterIssuer` applied
alongside cert-manager fails on the first reconcile — its CRD is not there yet
— and Flux retries the whole kustomization rather than the single object.

Almost everything a running workload reads comes from **OpenBao**, through an
`ExternalSecret` beside the manifests that consume it. The values are written
into the vault by the `openbao_secrets` Ansible role; this repository names the
path and never holds the value.

Three things are sealed into Git instead, and only three, because each is
needed before the vault can be reached at all: the registry credential Flux
pulls charts with, the CA key pair cert-manager issues from — including the
certificate on the vault's own ingress — and the vault's unseal keys.

Every `SealedSecret` here is generated at play time by `kubeseal`, from
plaintext held in `~/.config/rke2lab/env.sh` on the controller and from the
intermediate CA key `ipa_sub_ca` writes there. None of it is authored by hand
and none of it is recovered by reading this repository.

That matters for the rebuild case specifically: ciphertext committed here is
only ever decryptable by the sealing key that produced it, so a cluster rebuilt
with a fresh Sealed Secrets key would find every secret in its own Git history
useless. Regenerating instead means the plaintext is the source and the
ciphertext is a build product.

1. Mirror its images into GitLab first, via the manifest in
   `ansible/inventory/group_vars/repo/artifacts.yml`. Declaring something here
   that the registry does not hold produces an `ImagePullBackOff` that looks
   like a cluster fault.
2. Add the manifests under the right layer — in the Ansible role's template
   tree, not here.
3. Run `ansible-playbook playbooks/gitops.yml`. Flux reconciles within the
   interval, or `flux reconcile kustomization infra-controllers --with-source`
   to do it now.
